	notes: [
		'At planning time host.ts had 885 lines built by tasks 6, ne, ah, sj, 1w, j1, tf, 2r, rw, w4, wb, 6f, et, 29 and 0x (git log -- src/host/host.ts; each commit has a Task: trailer). Read those task notes before cutting: they explain races (wb: the amend deferred until Running.done settles; tf: retries) that a naive move can break.',
		'Calls go through module objects (host.x(), not x()) so hot patches work. When a function moves, tests that patch host.<fn> must patch its new object, or they silently stop patching anything; grep the tests for `host.` assignments.',
		'Two host modules may import each other only if neither touches the other at import time (tasks/README.md).',
		'The cut (by owned state and callers): host.ts keeps connections, command dispatch (act), ids, snapshots, broadcast and lifecycle (init, quitting, reset); status.ts owns the per-session state map (stateOf, transition, settle, inboxOf); prompts.ts is what the user sends (submit, amend, inbox delivery, next, drain, resume, reply, drafts sent); turns.ts owns running turns (state.running, stream, start, runTurn, ask, stop, recover, waitOut); slash.ts runs slash commands (command, context, change, runCommand, models, output). host.reset still clears turns.state.running and status.state.states, since tests reset through it.',
		'Patch points moved: tests stub turns.stream (was host.stream) and prompts.next / prompts.submit. `status` shadows the DOM global of the same name, so a file that forgets to import it type-checks as window.status (a string) and fails at .stateOf.',
		'host.test.ts was split into host.test.ts, turns.test.ts and prompts.test.ts sharing host-fixture.test.ts (named *.test.ts so the size and import-side-effect rules skip it; call useHost() to register its hooks). host-commands, host-retry and host-approval tests became slash.test.ts, turns-retry.test.ts and turns-approval.test.ts.'
